= Introduction<intro>

== Motivation
  
Artificial intelligence regarding deep learning architectures are currently beeing developed quite fast this time and are optimized more and more. With the introduction of transformer models user-oriented applications to generate text, audio and videos or identify everdays sequences featuring trainable logic. 

From an early stage psychological approaches are showing fixed behavioral patterns when showing emotions and sentiments including face and voice actions regarding semantic and syntax. Basic math and machine learning algorithms were able to classify some information and identify emotion thus neural networks are bringing more performance, better results in less time. The introduced transformers are infamous for their contextual understanding as well as very good and consistent outcomes.

This leads to the question if already developed applications like ChatGPT or in general approaches based on deep learning are able to recognize emotion from video, audio and text in the context of static interviews and real-life excerpts. The goal of this thesis is to compare state of the art models and architectures mostly focused on transformers which are present in every modal.

